From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fardjad Davari <fardjad.davari2@jumbo.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 23:42:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix: isolate release installer PATH opt-in

X-Jcode-Patch-Intent: keep release installer shell PATH changes opt-in
X-Jcode-Patch-Kind: personal-release-installer
X-Jcode-Patch-Depends-On: none

Patch intent:
Keep source release installation non-invasive by default, while supporting
explicit persistent PATH configuration through either --configure-path or the
stable JCODE_CONFIGURE_PATH automation interface.

Why it exists:
Installing a release must not silently edit shell startup files. Argument
parsing, truthy environment handling, and the sole PATH mutation gate live in a
focused helper so upstream installer changes conflict locally and visibly.
Unknown options fail, --fast remains order-independent, and false environment
values cannot override an explicit command-line opt-in. The helper is not a
replacement for the existing configure_path implementation.

Upstream integration points:
scripts/install_release.sh sources scripts/lib/install_release_policy.sh before
resolving the repository and delegates argument parsing and final PATH policy to
it. scripts/test_install_release_policy.sh covers parsing, ordering, duplicates,
unknown arguments, environment truthiness, and the no-source default invariant.
scripts/test_install_release_metadata.sh retains end-to-end default and opt-in
coverage, while README.md documents the stable user and automation contract.

Update guidance:
Preserve one policy gate before any shell startup mutation. Keep no arguments as
opt-out, accept --fast and --configure-path in either order, reject unknown
options, and treat only 1, true, yes, and on case-insensitively as environment
opt-ins. Keep the focused policy test separate from broad installer fixtures so
upstream metadata changes do not obscure policy regressions.

Validation:
bash scripts/test_install_release_policy.sh
bash scripts/test_install_release_metadata.sh
